10:32:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: GPS satellites don’t “serve” users individually like cell towers do. They continuously broadcast the same timing and orbit information to everyone within view. Your phone, car, or GPS receiver mostly works passively: 1. Each satellite broadcasts a precise timestamp and its current orbital position. 2. Your receiver listens to signals from several satellites—usually at least 4. 3. It measures how long each signal took to arrive. 4. From those distance estimates, it calculates its own position and time. Because receivers only listen and do not need to transmit a request back to the satellites, adding millions of users does not meaningfully increase the satellites’ workload. One satellite broadcast can be used by essentially unlimited receivers at once, much like millions of radios can listen to the same radio station. There also are more than just a “handful”: the GPS constellation typically has around 30 operational satellites, arranged so that most places on Earth can see several at any time. Crowds can still affect GPS indirectly—for example through radio interference, buildings blocking signals, atmospheric effects, or congestion in phone networks used for map downloads—but the core satellite positioning signal itself does not get overloaded by many users.
